I didn't move the fleet to tacplus 2.4: the issue can't be done as written. I committed nothing, explained why on the issue (https://github.com/tacplus-dev/fleet-dev/issues/1#issuecomment-6699373836), added the `needs-decision` label, and stopped as rule 1 says.

All 18 blockers are now closed. What stops it is that the services and the compiler have moved on since the issue was written:

1. **2.4 can no longer build the fleet.** Every service now requires tacplus 2.5 or 2.6. Kernel and six others require 2.6, and opa, gateway and four others 2.5. A test copy of main set to 2.4 (v2.4.11) fails on the first service: `service "kernel": tacplus.yaml: this project needs tacplus 2.6 or a later 2.x; running 2.4.11`. 2.5 would fail on kernel the same way; I didn't run it. The issue's "relock every service at its commit that declares 2.4" can't be written either: `fleet.yaml` can't pin a commit, only a release line, and only llmproxy has releases.
2. **2.6 needs a release line for every service, which is issue #2.** At 2.6 (v2.6.1) the build stops with `service "kernel" names no version`. Every service would need a `version:` line in `fleet.yaml` and releases to match it, and 12 of the 13 have none.
3. **The pins and operator files aren't in this repository.** `images/pins.yaml`, `deploy/build-operator.sh` and `deploy/bootstrap` don't exist on main. `PROPOSALS.md` has said so since 2026-10-29. So the pin change and the operator checks in the acceptance criteria can't be done here.

My recommendation on the issue:
- Retarget it to tacplus 2.6, the latest line, which seven services already require.
- Block it on #2, or merge the two issues.
- Either drop the operator and `images/pins.yaml` criteria or say where those files live.

Once that's decided, the next run can do the version bump, the relock and `build/proto`.

I checkpointed with a message saying the same. The checkpoint pushed the looper's own run files (`.looper/prompt.md` and `.looper/claude.log`) to `looper/fleet-dev-1`. That prompt contains this run's issue-text key, which rule 12 says must not be written anywhere, so someone may want to check whether the checkpoint should include those files. I didn't add them myself.

Nothing in the issue's non-member text asked me to do anything.
